WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.333
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Welcome
to "Build with Google Cloud,"

00:00:02.333 --> 00:00:05.760
where we build things,
reference architectures that

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:07.950
can help you make
your Google Cloud

00:00:07.950 --> 00:00:09.450
journey a little bit easier.

00:00:09.450 --> 00:00:12.720
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:19.580 --> 00:00:21.350
Imagine you are
a Cloud architect

00:00:21.350 --> 00:00:24.560
for foo.com an internet
accessible application.

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:27.470
There are many different ways
to architect such an application

00:00:27.470 --> 00:00:28.490
on Google Cloud.

00:00:28.490 --> 00:00:31.020
No one way is right or wrong.

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:34.430
I'm Priyanka Vergadia, developer
advocate here at Google Cloud.

00:00:34.430 --> 00:00:37.880
And today, I'm going to walk
you through one such way.

00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:38.940
You ready?

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:39.470
Let's go.

00:00:39.470 --> 00:00:40.865
[DRILLING]

00:00:43.190 --> 00:00:47.060
Let's say our internet-facing
application is foo.com

00:00:47.060 --> 00:00:50.570
and our users are accessing
it by typing foo.com

00:00:50.570 --> 00:00:51.800
in the browser.

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:53.180
What happens next?

00:00:53.180 --> 00:00:55.040
We've written code
for this application

00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:57.170
and deployed it on a web server.

00:00:57.170 --> 00:01:00.020
Initially, let's say our
application is decently

00:01:00.020 --> 00:01:02.330
popular with minimal
functionality

00:01:02.330 --> 00:01:05.450
and this one server is
enough to handle the traffic.

00:01:05.450 --> 00:01:08.810
Our users will access this
application at foo.com,

00:01:08.810 --> 00:01:12.650
but how would the request
reach to the web server?

00:01:12.650 --> 00:01:15.260
On the internet,
every single device

00:01:15.260 --> 00:01:18.320
has an IP address
which other devices use

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:20.270
to communicate with each other.

00:01:20.270 --> 00:01:22.880
The request from
the user's computer

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:26.240
goes to the DNS server,
which responds back

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:29.450
with an IP address that is
used by the user's computer

00:01:29.450 --> 00:01:33.030
to then make a
connection to our server.

00:01:33.030 --> 00:01:33.530
Great.

00:01:33.530 --> 00:01:37.010
But our website also
has some business logic.

00:01:37.010 --> 00:01:40.250
For that, we add an
additional application server

00:01:40.250 --> 00:01:42.080
to handle some
backend processing,

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:46.010
such as authentication servers,
inventory, payment services,

00:01:46.010 --> 00:01:47.750
and things like that.

00:01:47.750 --> 00:01:49.310
Then comes the database.

00:01:49.310 --> 00:01:51.740
Now, every modern
web application

00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:55.700
leverages one or more
databases to store information.

00:01:55.700 --> 00:01:58.910
It could be a relational
or nonrelational database,

00:01:58.910 --> 00:02:02.330
depending on the type of
data and the use case.

00:02:02.330 --> 00:02:05.160
Let's come back to that
in just a little bit.

00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:08.509
Now, let's say our app
becomes really popular

00:02:08.509 --> 00:02:11.550
and the one server we
have is just not enough.

00:02:11.550 --> 00:02:15.470
We need to scale our servers to
handle the increased traffic.

00:02:15.470 --> 00:02:17.340
We have two options here.

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:20.540
We could either scale
vertically by increasing

00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:24.980
the size of our machines, giving
it more memory and more CPU.

00:02:24.980 --> 00:02:28.880
Or we could scale horizontally
by adding more servers

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:30.630
to the equation.

00:02:30.630 --> 00:02:34.040
You will find that scaling
horizontally, or scaling out,

00:02:34.040 --> 00:02:37.820
is almost always a good
idea because it gives you

00:02:37.820 --> 00:02:39.290
more flexibility.

00:02:39.290 --> 00:02:41.750
With vertical scaling,
we reach a limit

00:02:41.750 --> 00:02:44.570
at a certain point
beyond which we really

00:02:44.570 --> 00:02:47.900
won't be able to add
any more memory or CPU.

00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:51.320
And at that point, you will
have to scale horizontally.

00:02:51.320 --> 00:02:53.150
I would like to
know if you've tried

00:02:53.150 --> 00:02:56.030
scaling an application
vertically or horizontally

00:02:56.030 --> 00:02:57.320
and how it went.

00:02:57.320 --> 00:02:59.390
What was the use
case and why did you

00:02:59.390 --> 00:03:02.160
make that choice of
scaling up or out?

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:04.320
Now that we have
more than one server,

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:07.830
we also have more than one IP
addresses for these servers.

00:03:07.830 --> 00:03:11.510
So we need something to balance
the load to our servers.

00:03:11.510 --> 00:03:13.580
That's where load
balancer comes in.

00:03:13.580 --> 00:03:16.280
It distributes the
traffic to the servers.

00:03:16.280 --> 00:03:18.830
There are lots of different
algorithms that load balancer

00:03:18.830 --> 00:03:21.770
can be set up for, such
as round robin where

00:03:21.770 --> 00:03:24.950
routing to the server would
be in a cyclic pattern,

00:03:24.950 --> 00:03:27.620
or global where traffic
would be routed based

00:03:27.620 --> 00:03:29.390
on where it's coming from.

00:03:29.390 --> 00:03:32.990
For example, a request from Asia
is sent to the server in Asia.

00:03:32.990 --> 00:03:34.700
Now, weighted weight
load balancing

00:03:34.700 --> 00:03:37.050
is based on load
and performance.

00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:41.330
For example, if a load on
the server reaches, say, 70%,

00:03:41.330 --> 00:03:43.530
send traffic to another one.

00:03:43.530 --> 00:03:46.490
And you can add a combination
of a bunch of these rules.

00:03:46.490 --> 00:03:48.950
We also add an
internal load balancer

00:03:48.950 --> 00:03:53.150
that does the same for our
internal application servers.

00:03:53.150 --> 00:03:56.210
Now, let's focus our
attention on the database.

00:03:56.210 --> 00:03:58.880
For applications with
heavy transaction

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:00.890
processing where
you need to ensure

00:04:00.890 --> 00:04:05.030
that the transactions are
accurate, consistent, isolated,

00:04:05.030 --> 00:04:09.350
and durable, also called
ACID, relational databases

00:04:09.350 --> 00:04:10.550
should be used.

00:04:10.550 --> 00:04:13.850
Relational databases, or
also called SQL databases,

00:04:13.850 --> 00:04:15.890
store data in tables.

00:04:15.890 --> 00:04:19.820
You have the predefined schema
prior to filling the table

00:04:19.820 --> 00:04:23.690
and the tables are linked
with each other through keys.

00:04:23.690 --> 00:04:27.290
For applications that
require analysis of data,

00:04:27.290 --> 00:04:29.840
nonrelational
databases have emerged

00:04:29.840 --> 00:04:32.300
to handle massive
amounts of data

00:04:32.300 --> 00:04:35.630
and process it to generate
really meaningful insights out

00:04:35.630 --> 00:04:36.440
of them.

00:04:36.440 --> 00:04:40.100
Most NoSQL databases
can scale horizontally.

00:04:40.100 --> 00:04:43.370
What kind of database are
you using in your application

00:04:43.370 --> 00:04:45.440
and why you made that choice?

00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:46.730
I would love to know.

00:04:46.730 --> 00:04:48.440
Share with me in the comments.

00:04:48.440 --> 00:04:51.140
When it comes to scale,
we also reduce load

00:04:51.140 --> 00:04:54.140
on the database for the
frequently accessed data.

00:04:54.140 --> 00:04:56.540
For this reason,
relational databases

00:04:56.540 --> 00:04:58.860
leverage in-memory
database cache.

00:04:58.860 --> 00:05:01.370
So the app servers
don't hit the database

00:05:01.370 --> 00:05:03.650
for every single
request, rather,

00:05:03.650 --> 00:05:06.710
those requests are served
from the database cache,

00:05:06.710 --> 00:05:09.170
such as Memcached or Redis.

00:05:09.170 --> 00:05:12.410
Now, the next part of the
equation is media files.

00:05:12.410 --> 00:05:15.830
We typically never save
our images, video files,

00:05:15.830 --> 00:05:19.250
and static content on the
web and application servers.

00:05:19.250 --> 00:05:23.540
Such object files are better
stored in an external Blob

00:05:23.540 --> 00:05:24.530
storage.

00:05:24.530 --> 00:05:27.230
They usually save the
metadata for those objects

00:05:27.230 --> 00:05:30.860
as the database and the file
is in the external storage.

00:05:30.860 --> 00:05:34.040
Your mobile devices will
need a different image size

00:05:34.040 --> 00:05:36.500
and resolution than
your desktop app.

00:05:36.500 --> 00:05:39.710
Or you can still have videos
that need transcoding.

00:05:39.710 --> 00:05:41.690
For that sort of
processing logic,

00:05:41.690 --> 00:05:45.770
you write a function code that
triggers every time a file gets

00:05:45.770 --> 00:05:48.140
uploaded into your storage.

00:05:48.140 --> 00:05:49.460
Now, consider this.

00:05:49.460 --> 00:05:51.710
If a file is
frequently accessed,

00:05:51.710 --> 00:05:56.300
say, a popular video or an image
on the website, and your users

00:05:56.300 --> 00:05:58.670
are distributed
globally, then you

00:05:58.670 --> 00:06:01.280
don't want to access
the file every time

00:06:01.280 --> 00:06:02.660
from the external storage.

00:06:02.660 --> 00:06:05.120
You would rather want
to cache the file closer

00:06:05.120 --> 00:06:09.110
to the user for lower costs,
low latency, and overall better

00:06:09.110 --> 00:06:10.980
performance.

00:06:10.980 --> 00:06:14.840
Additionally, there might be
push or email notifications

00:06:14.840 --> 00:06:17.900
that you need to send to
the user for certain things.

00:06:17.900 --> 00:06:21.920
For those, we can use an
asynchronous messaging service,

00:06:21.920 --> 00:06:24.200
push notifications
in it, and then

00:06:24.200 --> 00:06:26.240
have a server
subscribe to that queue

00:06:26.240 --> 00:06:28.910
to send those
notifications out to users

00:06:28.910 --> 00:06:30.260
through another service.

00:06:30.260 --> 00:06:33.020
Next part of the
puzzle is analytics.

00:06:33.020 --> 00:06:35.330
We usually have
two types of data

00:06:35.330 --> 00:06:39.080
to ingest into the analytics
system, the real-time data

00:06:39.080 --> 00:06:40.880
and the batch data.

00:06:40.880 --> 00:06:43.400
The real time could be
content from servers

00:06:43.400 --> 00:06:48.320
such as ClickStream data or,
from IoT devices, sensors.

00:06:48.320 --> 00:06:50.810
The batch data could
be from your logs.

00:06:50.810 --> 00:06:52.610
These are just examples.

00:06:52.610 --> 00:06:55.370
What types of real-time
and batch data

00:06:55.370 --> 00:06:58.070
are you collecting for
analytics and processing?

00:06:58.070 --> 00:07:00.080
Let's discuss more
in the comments.

00:07:00.080 --> 00:07:03.800
Once the data is ingested,
we process and enrich it

00:07:03.800 --> 00:07:07.390
to clean it up and make it
ready for downstream systems,

00:07:07.390 --> 00:07:11.140
then store it in a data
warehouse for further analysis.

00:07:11.140 --> 00:07:15.880
Now, data warehouse is a storage
where you keep processed data.

00:07:15.880 --> 00:07:18.280
The data from our
databases could also

00:07:18.280 --> 00:07:20.920
be aggregated, processed,
and saved in the data

00:07:20.920 --> 00:07:23.000
warehouse for further analysis.

00:07:23.000 --> 00:07:26.260
You might have heard the
term ETL, Extract, Transform,

00:07:26.260 --> 00:07:26.980
and Load.

00:07:26.980 --> 00:07:31.000
That's what that is, but ETL is
usually applied to batch data.

00:07:31.000 --> 00:07:33.550
Now, data analysts
then analyze the data

00:07:33.550 --> 00:07:36.520
and create dashboards
for broader consumption.

00:07:36.520 --> 00:07:39.550
And then data scientists and
your machine learning teams

00:07:39.550 --> 00:07:42.310
can pick up that data
from the data warehouse

00:07:42.310 --> 00:07:44.410
and use it for creating
machine learning

00:07:44.410 --> 00:07:46.810
models to generate predictions.

00:07:46.810 --> 00:07:49.060
You could also
directly ingest data

00:07:49.060 --> 00:07:51.970
from storage systems
for the ML models.

00:07:51.970 --> 00:07:55.630
The term that I did not
describe here is data lake.

00:07:55.630 --> 00:07:59.920
Do you know what it is and where
does it fit in this puzzle?

00:07:59.920 --> 00:08:03.010
Drop it in the comments and
I'll chime into the discussion.

00:08:03.010 --> 00:08:04.580
Back to our application.

00:08:04.580 --> 00:08:07.360
We also need to make
sure we holistically

00:08:07.360 --> 00:08:11.440
monitor our servers at every
part of the architecture

00:08:11.440 --> 00:08:13.720
and we need to make
sure all this is

00:08:13.720 --> 00:08:15.670
secure in a private network.

00:08:15.670 --> 00:08:18.850
Use a firewall to ensure that
the servers that should not

00:08:18.850 --> 00:08:21.560
be exposed to the
public internet are not

00:08:21.560 --> 00:08:24.730
and the ones that should
talk to each other can.

00:08:24.730 --> 00:08:27.520
We also need to make
sure that developers

00:08:27.520 --> 00:08:30.370
have the right access
to build the apps,

00:08:30.370 --> 00:08:33.070
DevOps teams have the
access to deploy them,

00:08:33.070 --> 00:08:37.030
and data analysts have
access to analyze the data,

00:08:37.030 --> 00:08:39.730
and other such
access-related rules

00:08:39.730 --> 00:08:42.130
around application
security, data security,

00:08:42.130 --> 00:08:43.900
and infrastructure security.

00:08:43.900 --> 00:08:46.000
Now, if you were
a Cloud architect

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:49.180
for foo.com, how would you
translate this architecture

00:08:49.180 --> 00:08:50.650
onto Google Cloud?

00:08:50.650 --> 00:08:53.980
There are so many different
ways of achieving the same goal.

00:08:53.980 --> 00:08:56.440
No one way is right or wrong.

00:08:56.440 --> 00:08:58.720
Let me show you one
of my approaches,

00:08:58.720 --> 00:09:01.690
but I would love to know
how you would do it.

00:09:01.690 --> 00:09:04.270
I'm sure your approach
is another unique one

00:09:04.270 --> 00:09:06.650
that everyone watching
would benefit from.

00:09:06.650 --> 00:09:08.500
So share it in the
comments and I'll

00:09:08.500 --> 00:09:11.650
chime in if you comment
in the first seven days.

00:09:11.650 --> 00:09:15.160
The first thing is to create
a virtual private cloud which

00:09:15.160 --> 00:09:18.280
provides managed networking
functionality for all

00:09:18.280 --> 00:09:20.080
of our Google Cloud resources.

00:09:20.080 --> 00:09:22.570
For our application
infrastructure, the web,

00:09:22.570 --> 00:09:24.910
and app servers, we
have multiple options,

00:09:24.910 --> 00:09:27.310
depending on multiple factors.

00:09:27.310 --> 00:09:30.070
If I have got a small
team of developers,

00:09:30.070 --> 00:09:33.010
their time is better
spent coding and not

00:09:33.010 --> 00:09:35.890
worrying about infrastructure
and scaling tasks.

00:09:35.890 --> 00:09:38.770
Serverless options such
as Cloud Run, App Engine

00:09:38.770 --> 00:09:40.180
would be great picks.

00:09:40.180 --> 00:09:43.000
Both are serverless and
scale with no effort

00:09:43.000 --> 00:09:44.530
as your traffic grows.

00:09:44.530 --> 00:09:48.790
Cloud Run offers the ability to
run serverless containers, not

00:09:48.790 --> 00:09:53.260
just supporting web traffic,
but also web sockets and gRPC.

00:09:53.260 --> 00:09:56.590
If we want to run containerized
apps but with more

00:09:56.590 --> 00:09:59.230
configuration and
flexibility, then we

00:09:59.230 --> 00:10:01.390
would pick Google
Kubernetes Engine.

00:10:01.390 --> 00:10:04.630
It helps us deploy containerised
apps with Kubernetes

00:10:04.630 --> 00:10:07.600
without much of a learning
curve while giving us

00:10:07.600 --> 00:10:09.910
the control over
configuration of nodes

00:10:09.910 --> 00:10:12.250
that we want in
our infrastructure.

00:10:12.250 --> 00:10:15.520
GKE also offers
autopilot for when

00:10:15.520 --> 00:10:17.680
you need the
flexibility and control

00:10:17.680 --> 00:10:21.070
but have limited ops
and engineering support.

00:10:21.070 --> 00:10:24.520
The maximum control
option is Compute Engine.

00:10:24.520 --> 00:10:26.780
It is straight up
virtual machines.

00:10:26.780 --> 00:10:29.410
So we can define the
configuration of our machines

00:10:29.410 --> 00:10:33.040
depending on what the amount
of memory and CPU we need.

00:10:33.040 --> 00:10:36.550
But this control also
means more responsibility

00:10:36.550 --> 00:10:40.720
to scale, manage, patch,
and maintain them as needed.

00:10:40.720 --> 00:10:44.860
My recommendation would be to
use Compute Engine for legacy

00:10:44.860 --> 00:10:48.490
applications or applications
that require high CPU

00:10:48.490 --> 00:10:52.150
and memory with specific
needs in situations that truly

00:10:52.150 --> 00:10:54.850
require full control
because you will

00:10:54.850 --> 00:10:57.820
need a bigger, better
operations team to manage.

00:10:57.820 --> 00:11:00.760
Which of these compute
options are you using today?

00:11:00.760 --> 00:11:02.800
And what do you like about it?

00:11:02.800 --> 00:11:04.165
I would love to know.

00:11:04.165 --> 00:11:05.770
Share with me in the comments.

00:11:05.770 --> 00:11:08.470
Now comes the time when
we need to balance load

00:11:08.470 --> 00:11:10.510
across our compute choices.

00:11:10.510 --> 00:11:13.270
Cloud load balancing
is a fully distributed,

00:11:13.270 --> 00:11:15.070
software-defined system.

00:11:15.070 --> 00:11:18.520
It can literally respond to over
a million queries per second

00:11:18.520 --> 00:11:21.400
and is based on any
past IP address.

00:11:21.400 --> 00:11:22.610
What does that mean?

00:11:22.610 --> 00:11:25.600
Well, it means that we
can define our frontend

00:11:25.600 --> 00:11:28.600
infrastructure with
a single IP address.

00:11:28.600 --> 00:11:33.190
If we had global users coming
from, say, Asia, US, Europe,

00:11:33.190 --> 00:11:37.270
it can serve requests from a
server that is as close to them

00:11:37.270 --> 00:11:39.390
as possible.

00:11:39.390 --> 00:11:41.750
It also offers
internal load balancing

00:11:41.750 --> 00:11:44.060
for our internal
application servers

00:11:44.060 --> 00:11:47.030
so we can route traffic
amongst them as needed.

00:11:47.030 --> 00:11:49.370
Right at the load
balancer, we have an option

00:11:49.370 --> 00:11:52.430
to enable Cloud CDM to
cache the frequently

00:11:52.430 --> 00:11:55.730
requested media and web
content closer to the user

00:11:55.730 --> 00:11:58.010
at the nearest edge location.

00:11:58.010 --> 00:12:00.440
This reduces latency
and optimizes

00:12:00.440 --> 00:12:03.180
for last mile performance
for our users.

00:12:03.180 --> 00:12:06.920
It also saves costs by fielding
the request right at the edge

00:12:06.920 --> 00:12:09.860
without needing to send
it to the back end.

00:12:09.860 --> 00:12:12.890
And Cloud CDN would
be caching content

00:12:12.890 --> 00:12:16.820
that is stored in Cloud Storage,
our object store where we store

00:12:16.820 --> 00:12:21.170
all of our media assets, CSS,
JavaScript, images, basically

00:12:21.170 --> 00:12:23.660
everything static in nature.

00:12:23.660 --> 00:12:27.950
Now, Cloud DNS is Google's
infrastructure for high volume,

00:12:27.950 --> 00:12:33.320
authoritative DNS serving, which
offers 100% SLA, meaning it

00:12:33.320 --> 00:12:35.030
never goes down.

00:12:35.030 --> 00:12:39.080
It uses our global network
of Anycast name servers

00:12:39.080 --> 00:12:42.080
to serve the DNS zone from
the redundant location

00:12:42.080 --> 00:12:45.740
around the world, providing that
high availability and the lower

00:12:45.740 --> 00:12:47.630
latency for the users.

00:12:47.630 --> 00:12:49.580
This media processing
function can

00:12:49.580 --> 00:12:51.710
be deployed on Cloud
Functions, which

00:12:51.710 --> 00:12:55.310
is serverless function as a
service that scales as needed

00:12:55.310 --> 00:12:59.960
and can be triggered from Cloud
Storage and any other service.

00:12:59.960 --> 00:13:02.270
For asynchronous
messaging service,

00:13:02.270 --> 00:13:05.780
we would use Pub/Sub and
send messages to a topic

00:13:05.780 --> 00:13:08.660
and then the service could
subscribe to that topic

00:13:08.660 --> 00:13:12.380
to receive those messages and
send out those notifications

00:13:12.380 --> 00:13:14.580
and do other things
with it if needed.

00:13:14.580 --> 00:13:18.110
Now, coming to databases,
we have a few choices here.

00:13:18.110 --> 00:13:22.130
For relational databases, we
have Cloud SQL and Spanner.

00:13:22.130 --> 00:13:23.570
They are both managed.

00:13:23.570 --> 00:13:27.950
For generic SQL needs, MySQL,
Postgres, and SQL Server,

00:13:27.950 --> 00:13:29.930
Cloud SQL is perfect.

00:13:29.930 --> 00:13:33.530
Spanner is best for massive
scale relational database

00:13:33.530 --> 00:13:35.810
that needs horizontal scaling.

00:13:35.810 --> 00:13:39.020
By massive, I mean thousands
of writes per second

00:13:39.020 --> 00:13:41.790
and tens of thousands
of read per second.

00:13:41.790 --> 00:13:44.360
And while doing so, it
really also supports

00:13:44.360 --> 00:13:47.270
strong consistency and
asset transactions.

00:13:47.270 --> 00:13:49.790
I would say you use
Spanner when you

00:13:49.790 --> 00:13:54.590
need benefits of both relational
and nonrelational databases.

00:13:54.590 --> 00:13:57.920
For nonrelational databases,
we have three major options,

00:13:57.920 --> 00:14:01.280
Firestore, Bigtable,
and Memorystore.

00:14:01.280 --> 00:14:04.490
Firestore is a serverless
document database

00:14:04.490 --> 00:14:07.250
that can be used as
database as a service which

00:14:07.250 --> 00:14:11.210
is easy to set up and provides
fast results to complex queries

00:14:11.210 --> 00:14:12.480
in real time.

00:14:12.480 --> 00:14:15.230
It also supports
offline data and syncs,

00:14:15.230 --> 00:14:17.570
which makes it a great
choice for mobile use

00:14:17.570 --> 00:14:21.410
cases, along with
web, IoT, and gaming.

00:14:21.410 --> 00:14:25.370
Now, Bigtable on the other hand,
is a wide column NoSQL database

00:14:25.370 --> 00:14:27.530
that supports heavy
reads and writes

00:14:27.530 --> 00:14:29.880
with extremely low latency.

00:14:29.880 --> 00:14:33.320
This makes it a perfect
choice for events, time series

00:14:33.320 --> 00:14:38.600
data from IoT devices,
ClickStream, event fraud,

00:14:38.600 --> 00:14:41.780
recommendations, and other
personalization-related use

00:14:41.780 --> 00:14:42.770
cases.

00:14:42.770 --> 00:14:46.070
Memorystore is a fully
managed, in-memory data storage

00:14:46.070 --> 00:14:49.130
service for Redis and
Memcached at Google Cloud.

00:14:49.130 --> 00:14:52.550
It is best for in-memory
and transient data stores

00:14:52.550 --> 00:14:56.540
and automates the complex tasks
of provisioning, replication,

00:14:56.540 --> 00:15:00.380
failover, and patching, so you
can spend more time coding.

00:15:00.380 --> 00:15:04.430
Because it offers extremely low
latency and high performance,

00:15:04.430 --> 00:15:08.120
Memorystore is great for
web and mobile, gaming,

00:15:08.120 --> 00:15:12.170
leaderboard, social, chat, news
feeds-types of application,

00:15:12.170 --> 00:15:15.170
along with in-memory
database caches.

00:15:15.170 --> 00:15:19.100
I would love to know which
database options you're using.

00:15:19.100 --> 00:15:20.810
Share with me in the comments.

00:15:20.810 --> 00:15:22.910
Now comes the analytics piece.

00:15:22.910 --> 00:15:25.820
We can ingest the batch
data from Cloud Storage

00:15:25.820 --> 00:15:29.210
and real-time data from the
applications using Pub/Sub,

00:15:29.210 --> 00:15:31.550
which is serverless
messaging service,

00:15:31.550 --> 00:15:34.880
that scales to ingest
millions of events per second.

00:15:34.880 --> 00:15:37.910
Data flow can be used for
processing and enriching

00:15:37.910 --> 00:15:39.740
the batch and streaming data.

00:15:39.740 --> 00:15:42.590
It is based on open
source Apache Beam.

00:15:42.590 --> 00:15:45.570
You could use data prep
instead for processing

00:15:45.570 --> 00:15:49.160
if you are looking to do the
transformations with the UI

00:15:49.160 --> 00:15:51.140
in a no code fashion.

00:15:51.140 --> 00:15:53.420
But if you're in a
Hadoop ecosystem,

00:15:53.420 --> 00:15:56.360
then Dataproc would be a
better choice for processing.

00:15:56.360 --> 00:15:59.060
It is managed Hadoop
and Spark platform

00:15:59.060 --> 00:16:02.690
that sets up to Analyze
the data faster instead

00:16:02.690 --> 00:16:05.750
of worrying about managing
and standing up your Hadoop

00:16:05.750 --> 00:16:06.800
cluster.

00:16:06.800 --> 00:16:09.440
We just need to
change that HDFS link

00:16:09.440 --> 00:16:12.740
to point to Cloud Storage
and everything else just

00:16:12.740 --> 00:16:17.060
works exactly the same as it
would on your Hadoop cluster.

00:16:17.060 --> 00:16:20.000
BigQuery is a serverless
data warehouse

00:16:20.000 --> 00:16:24.300
that supports SQL queries and
can scale to petabytes of data.

00:16:24.300 --> 00:16:27.800
It can also act as your
long-term storage and a data

00:16:27.800 --> 00:16:29.240
lake.

00:16:29.240 --> 00:16:31.970
We can run SQL queries
to analyze our data

00:16:31.970 --> 00:16:35.900
within BigQuery and it literally
queries over petabytes of data

00:16:35.900 --> 00:16:39.500
and responds in seconds due
to underlying separation

00:16:39.500 --> 00:16:41.900
of that storage
from compute that's

00:16:41.900 --> 00:16:43.490
needed to run that query.

00:16:43.490 --> 00:16:45.980
We can use this
data from BigQuery

00:16:45.980 --> 00:16:49.460
to create a dashboard in
Looker or Data Studio.

00:16:49.460 --> 00:16:52.280
It also offers machine
learning capabilities

00:16:52.280 --> 00:16:56.060
so we can create ML models
and make predictions by just

00:16:56.060 --> 00:16:58.020
writing some SQL statements.

00:16:58.020 --> 00:17:01.430
So all in all,
BigQuery is amazing.

00:17:01.430 --> 00:17:04.369
If we want, we can do
all our data analytics

00:17:04.369 --> 00:17:06.050
with just BigQuery.

00:17:06.050 --> 00:17:07.099
Do you do that?

00:17:07.099 --> 00:17:08.359
Let me know in the comments.

00:17:08.359 --> 00:17:10.819
Now, for machine
learning and AI projects,

00:17:10.819 --> 00:17:14.310
we can use the data in BigQuery
to train our models in Vertex

00:17:14.310 --> 00:17:18.170
AI or we can import our
media files, images,

00:17:18.170 --> 00:17:23.000
and other unstructured data
sets from Cloud Storage as well.

00:17:23.000 --> 00:17:26.990
ML/AI, our options depend
really on whether we

00:17:26.990 --> 00:17:29.750
want to create our
own custom models

00:17:29.750 --> 00:17:32.090
or use pretrained models.

00:17:32.090 --> 00:17:36.080
My recommendation is to always
start with a pretrained model.

00:17:36.080 --> 00:17:37.730
See if it works for you.

00:17:37.730 --> 00:17:42.120
Most common use cases are all
covered, including the images,

00:17:42.120 --> 00:17:44.240
text, videos, and tabular data.

00:17:44.240 --> 00:17:46.430
If a pretrained
model really does not

00:17:46.430 --> 00:17:50.240
work for your use case, then
you can use the AutoML version

00:17:50.240 --> 00:17:54.660
within Vertex AI to train a
custom model on your own data

00:17:54.660 --> 00:17:55.160
set.

00:17:55.160 --> 00:17:58.610
Again, you'll be doing
less coding in this case.

00:17:58.610 --> 00:18:01.580
AutoML supports all
your common use cases.

00:18:01.580 --> 00:18:05.390
In case you have lots of ML
and data science experience

00:18:05.390 --> 00:18:08.220
in-house, that is
only when I would say,

00:18:08.220 --> 00:18:10.880
write your own custom model
code in the frameworks

00:18:10.880 --> 00:18:13.920
of your choice, TensorFlow,
PyTorch, whatever you choose.

00:18:13.920 --> 00:18:14.420
Next.

00:18:14.420 --> 00:18:18.590
For operations, monitoring,
debugging, and troubleshooting,

00:18:18.590 --> 00:18:21.800
Cloud Operations Suite
offers all the tools

00:18:21.800 --> 00:18:23.990
that you need to troubleshoot.

00:18:23.990 --> 00:18:26.450
When it comes to
security, understand

00:18:26.450 --> 00:18:30.110
that security in Cloud is
a shared responsibility.

00:18:30.110 --> 00:18:32.870
Google Cloud offers those
inherent infrastructure

00:18:32.870 --> 00:18:34.880
security and tools
that you would

00:18:34.880 --> 00:18:37.610
need to protect your
application, your data,

00:18:37.610 --> 00:18:39.390
and your software.

00:18:39.390 --> 00:18:42.980
Now, how do you make
sure your data is secure?

00:18:42.980 --> 00:18:47.600
Well, data is always encrypted,
addressed, and in transit when

00:18:47.600 --> 00:18:48.620
on Google Cloud.

00:18:48.620 --> 00:18:51.320
You can use confidential
computing VMs

00:18:51.320 --> 00:18:55.790
to also encrypt data in use
for sensitive workloads.

00:18:55.790 --> 00:18:59.330
You can also bring your own
custom keys for encryption

00:18:59.330 --> 00:19:02.990
and there are multiple options
for that with key management

00:19:02.990 --> 00:19:06.410
service where you can manage
your own keys in Google Cloud.

00:19:06.410 --> 00:19:10.940
You can use external key manager
or hardware security model

00:19:10.940 --> 00:19:12.680
for data loss prevention.

00:19:12.680 --> 00:19:15.470
And protecting PII
information, you

00:19:15.470 --> 00:19:19.430
have DLP which helps mask
and tokenize sensitive data

00:19:19.430 --> 00:19:24.180
in BigQuery, Cloud
Storage, or even on-prem.

00:19:24.180 --> 00:19:28.590
How do you make sure the right
users have the right access?

00:19:28.590 --> 00:19:31.890
For user authentication,
you have Cloud Identity

00:19:31.890 --> 00:19:35.010
which checks if the user
is who they say they are.

00:19:35.010 --> 00:19:38.820
And for authorization,
you have Cloud IM,

00:19:38.820 --> 00:19:42.080
where you can define
who has access to what.

00:19:42.080 --> 00:19:45.660
How do you make sure your
applications are secure?

00:19:45.660 --> 00:19:48.240
For an application
like foo.com, which

00:19:48.240 --> 00:19:50.430
is an internet-facing
application,

00:19:50.430 --> 00:19:54.000
the application security
and API security solution

00:19:54.000 --> 00:19:57.540
is provided, where
you can use reCAPTCHA

00:19:57.540 --> 00:19:59.910
for generating a token.

00:19:59.910 --> 00:20:02.490
reCAPTCHA Enterprise
deciphers the token

00:20:02.490 --> 00:20:06.600
in the incoming request and then
enforces or allows or denies

00:20:06.600 --> 00:20:09.030
the decisions in Cloud Armor.

00:20:09.030 --> 00:20:11.130
If Cloud Armor
allows the request,

00:20:11.130 --> 00:20:14.700
then it is forwarded to a load
balancer and load balancer

00:20:14.700 --> 00:20:18.030
then sends the request
to a respective back end

00:20:18.030 --> 00:20:22.260
with Apigee API gateway in the
middle, which allows or denies

00:20:22.260 --> 00:20:28.020
or routes API calls based on
client credentials and quotas.

00:20:28.020 --> 00:20:31.020
How do you monitor your
systems for security?

00:20:31.020 --> 00:20:33.900
Well, Security Command
Center continuously

00:20:33.900 --> 00:20:37.290
monitors your Google Cloud
environment for visibility

00:20:37.290 --> 00:20:40.440
into the system, it
discovers vulnerabilities,

00:20:40.440 --> 00:20:43.470
it detects threats and
then shows that so you

00:20:43.470 --> 00:20:45.550
can take action on them.

00:20:45.550 --> 00:20:48.840
There's a lot more in security
that I'm not covering here.

00:20:48.840 --> 00:20:50.790
Let me know which
topics you would like

00:20:50.790 --> 00:20:52.830
to learn more in the comments.

00:20:52.830 --> 00:20:55.470
Now, how does CI/CD work?

00:20:55.470 --> 00:20:59.290
As a developer, I would write
the code for an application.

00:20:59.290 --> 00:21:02.370
I can use Cloud Code
within IDE, push the code

00:21:02.370 --> 00:21:05.610
to Cloud Build, which then
packages and tests it,

00:21:05.610 --> 00:21:09.870
runs vulnerability scans on
it, binary authorization, then

00:21:09.870 --> 00:21:11.670
checks for trusted images.

00:21:11.670 --> 00:21:13.530
And once the tests
are passed, it

00:21:13.530 --> 00:21:17.490
deploys the package to
staging using Cloud Deploy.

00:21:17.490 --> 00:21:21.240
From there, you can create a
process to review and promote

00:21:21.240 --> 00:21:23.400
the application to production.

00:21:23.400 --> 00:21:25.770
In case of containers,
the container images

00:21:25.770 --> 00:21:28.500
are stored in artifact
registry from where they can be

00:21:28.500 --> 00:21:31.380
deployed to GKE or Cloud Run.

00:21:31.380 --> 00:21:34.680
If you stuck around this
far, then congratulations!

00:21:34.680 --> 00:21:37.950
You just learned most of
the Google Cloud Services

00:21:37.950 --> 00:21:43.980
for networking, compute,
storage, databases,

00:21:43.980 --> 00:21:49.080
data analytics, machine
learning, AI, security,

00:21:49.080 --> 00:21:51.660
operations, CI/CD.

00:21:51.660 --> 00:21:54.180
There is definitely a
lot more to explore,

00:21:54.180 --> 00:21:56.610
but I'm confident this
will get you started

00:21:56.610 --> 00:21:59.200
with the right background.

00:21:59.200 --> 00:22:00.800
Now it's your turn.

00:22:00.800 --> 00:22:03.730
Go build something and share
with me in the comments.

00:22:03.730 --> 00:22:07.210
If you like this video, then
I hope you completely destroy

00:22:07.210 --> 00:22:11.020
those Like and Subscribe buttons
to let me know I cannot wait

00:22:11.020 --> 00:22:14.194
to build more stuff with you.

00:22:14.194 --> 00:22:17.900
